// Quark on Push: a string and pad machine beside a mono synth.
// An overlay shipped with Pung (Quark ships no push_ui.js).
//
// Quark is built as two instruments in one case, the way the string machines with a synth section
// were: the ATMOS layer (up to twelve voices: a divide-down string machine whose 16', 8', 4' and
// 2' registers run phase-locked off one divider, or a wavetable, or 3-op FM; its filter, its
// tri-phase ensemble) and the MELODY layer (a mono/legato/poly voice with two oscillators and a
// 4-pole ladder), then one multi-effect and a shimmer reverb over both. The front panel is that
// case: Atmos left, Melody right, the output last, each section one segment. The compartments are
// the module's own pages, each with the picture of what its knobs do together:
//
//   PANEL          the whole instrument: the registration (the stops the Variation knob has
//                  pulled, the wave they sum to), the ensemble's three delay taps, the filter, the
//                  amp envelope; the Melody's wave, ladder and envelope; the mix and the space
//   ATMOS          the register points along the Variation knob (violin to choir on Strings), the
//                  frames of a wavetable, the FM recipe; where Motion and Var Env take Variation
//   ATMOS FILTER   the response (Pung's, or the module's own curve where Pung has no such type),
//                  where the mod envelope takes the cutoff, both envelopes
//   DRIVE / WIDTH  the drive's transfer curve and where it sits; the chord's detune opening
//                  over the bloom time; the unison spread
//   MOTION         both LFOs and what each moves; the ensemble's taps
//   LAYER          the two sample sets as their outlines, the blend, where the note starts
//   MELODY         the two oscillators, the Variation macro (PWM, tremolo, vibrato), the envelope
//   MELODY FILTER  the ladder, where the envelope takes it, the delay's echoes
//   ARP            the notes the arp plays from a three-note chord, its rhythm
//   MIX / EFFECT   both layers through the effect and the reverb, as the routing switches say
//   PLAY           the keyboard: which layer plays which key
//
// Every value curve comes from Quark's own source (src/dsp, and src/ui/common.js, which mirrors
// it): envelope times and their bends, cutoff in notes, the ensemble's taps, the drive curve,
// the detune pattern, the reverb's times. Its tables (register points, wavetables, the Layer's
// sample outlines, the arp's patterns) are generated from tools/gen.py and tools/data below.
// ---- Quark's own tables, generated from the module's source (tools/gen.py, tools/data):
// register points, FM recipes, chord-stack frames, the Layer's sample outlines, the arp's
// patterns and rhythms, and every wave-bank table (64 samples a frame; Amino: 16 of its 61 waves)
